From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan <ryan@esterlines.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 10:29:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] LibJS: Don't keep the functions JIT code checks callees
 against alive

JIT code that checks a call's callee against the function the call site
saw, to inline it or call it directly, kept that function alive for as
long as the code lived, and with it the function's environment and
everything that holds. A site that saw a closure once kept it after
nothing else did: pages that make new closures on every render, as
React does, kept old closures and through their environments whole DOM
trees they had already removed.

The compiler now lists those functions as the code's guard cells,
apart from the cells it embeds, unless it also needs them otherwise.
The code keeps them alive only while a frame runs it, as the callee
does then, and is discarded during the collection that finds one of
them dead, before another cell can take its address and pass the
check. The discard isn't a fruitless one: the recompile checks against
the functions alive by then.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
